(2. "Am I a Caregiver?" Recognition Tool) Tj
T*
(Many people provide significant care without identifying themselves as caregivers. Recognizing) Tj
T*
(the) Tj
T*
(role matters because support is easier to seek when the work being done is visible.) Tj
T*
([ ] I manage or help coordinate appointments.) Tj
T*
([ ] I help with medications or treatment routines.) Tj
T*
([ ] I provide transportation because of the person's health or disability.) Tj
T*
([ ] I help with meals, household tasks, personal care, mobility, or other daily needs.) Tj
T*
([ ] I monitor safety or watch for changes in symptoms, behaviour, cognition, or functioning.) Tj
T*
([ ] I advocate with healthcare, disability, insurance, social-service, or community systems.) Tj
T*
([ ] I provide regular emotional reassurance or crisis support.) Tj
T*
([ ] I have taken on responsibilities the other person used to manage.) Tj
T*
([ ] My schedule, employment, finances, family responsibilities, or social life have changed) Tj
T*
(because of) Tj
T*
(the care I provide.) Tj
T*
(If several of these describe your life, you may be functioning as a caregiver even if) Tj
T*
(you have never used that word.) Tj
T*
(Recognizing the role does not change your relationship with the person you support. It may) Tj
T*
(simply make it easier to recognize that you also deserve information, preparation, and support.) Tj
T*
